--TEST--
A `Core\Heap::pop` whose comparator throws while the rest is re-ordered frees the element it had already taken, so a request that catches the throw a thousand times holds no more memory than one that never threw — `rule:errors/on-limit`, docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Heap::pop
final class Order {
    public static bool $refuse = false;

    public static function byText(string $a, string $b): int {
        if (Order::$refuse) {
            throw new LogicError('no');
        }
        return Core\Str::compare($a, $b);
    }
}

// Each round pushes three fresh 100 KB texts and pops with a comparator that
// throws once the root has been taken. A pop that kept its reference to the
// taken text would hold 100 KB per round, and 1000 rounds is past the 64 MB
// ceiling set here.
Core\Config::set('memory', '64M');
int $threw = 0;
int $round = 0;
while ($round < 1000) {
    var $heap = new Core\Heap<string>(Order::byText(...));
    Order::$refuse = false;
    $heap->push(Core\Str::repeat('c', 100000));
    $heap->push(Core\Str::repeat('a', 100000));
    $heap->push(Core\Str::repeat('b', 100000));
    Order::$refuse = true;
    try {
        $heap->pop();
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        $threw = $threw + 1;
    }
    Order::$refuse = false;
    $round = $round + 1;
}
echo "threw ", $threw, "\n";
--EXPECT--
threw 1000
